since the start of the pandemic physical  retailers have gotten crushed given the  fear of mingling with the public the  appeal of physical locations has  dwindled over the past year causing  dozens of legacy players to file for  bankruptcy including jcpenney j crew  century 21 guitar center pier 1 and many  more  but one retailer who has not only  survived the pandemic but has gone  parabolic is dillard's  since the pandemic lows dillards was up  a maximum of  1409 percent  that's nearly three times their previous  peak and nearly 100 more than tesla's  parabolic run not only has their stock  gone to the moon but dillards also has  the fundamentals to back up this run the  alerts has been posting record profits  and their p e ratio is still under 10.  so here's how one of the oldest and  slowest moving companies was able to  outperform even the most innovative  companies in the world since the  pandemic started  if you're not familiar with dillard's  they're a very popular department store  in the u.s who specializes in clothing  and fashion while i'm sure they have  their own fan base that won't shop  anywhere else as far as the general  public is concerned dillards is just not  a department store like macy's or  nordstrom this isn't necessarily a bad  thing though as most of the competition  doesn't have anything special going for  them on the consumer side either  but unlike much of the competition the  alerts has figured out a way to address  this issue from the business side you  see one of the biggest shortfalls of  running a retail store is the massive  overhead that comes along with each  store you have to pay for leases  maintenance and dozens of employees  regardless of whether you make sales or  not and the given that retail stores  don't have the highest net margins even  the smallest downturn in demand can  leave you unprofitable this isn't  anything new though and alerts has been  strategically combating their shortfall  by owning their stores as of 2011 the  alert zoned 241 out of their 309 stores  many of which they transferred into a  real estate investment trust or read and  today this number is simply even higher  with the dillard only 90 of their retail  space just like us mortals owning large  amounts of real estate as a retailer is  a massive advantage your monthly  payments are building equity your real  estate is appreciating you get tax  write-outs for interest and depreciation  and most importantly you have full  control over your property if a downturn  like the pandemic does hit and you need  to close some stores you can cash in on  your equity or you can become a landlord  yourself on the other hand if you're  releasing you're gonna have to negotiate  and release early and probably pay hefty  fines  all of the most successful retailers  today including walmart and the home  depot owned most of their stores and  they've all been taking the real estate  side of the business extremely seriously  for quite some time now on the flip side  most of the dying retailers don't own  their real estate or they were late to  the game jcpenney for example didn't  form a read until they filed for  bankruptcy last year clearly owning  their stores has been a great advantage  for dillard's but this isn't their only  cost-saving measure either  another way dillards has been able to  establish some stability in the  cutthroat retail industry is leaning up  the business historically dillard's has  been known for being a higher-end  retailer carrying brands like calvin  klein coach ralph lauren and michael  kors but over the past few years the  alerts have focused on appealing to the  middle class and lower income families  as well through their clearance stores  as the name suggests these clearance  stores specialize in selling items that  weren't able to be sold in regular  dealers and these stores have some of  the best deals out there before sending  merchandise to these clearance stores  the alerts tries to price these items 60  off at their regular stores and these  items only make their way to clearance  stores if they still don't sell this  means that only the most undesirable  stuff make their way to clearance  centers but on the bright side these  items are often 70 to 80 cheaper by the  time they make it there delirius  currently operates 30 clearance centers  across the us which has really expanded  it to its target audience and helped  them survive a time period in which many  consumers were more frugal than ever  before aside from appealing to lean  customers the alerts have become much  leaner themselves the alerts owns 100 of  their corporate headquarters  distribution centers and fulfillment  facilities which has minimized overhead  the alerts has also been strategically  closing a handful of stores that weren't  performing as well thanks to these  cost-cutting measures the alerts didn't  have to furlough or lay off any  employees during the pandemic and their  balance sheet has been remarkably  healthy their debt to equity ratio  currently stands at 0.4 meaning that  they have more equity than debt overall  also their long-term debt is only 599  million dollars and their next payment  isn't due till january of 2023.  considering internet income over the  past 12 months itself is 608 million  dollars dillard should not have any  trouble handling this debt as you can  see the alerts has really strong  fundamentals but fundamentals alone  aren't responsible for dillard's  exponential rise  some of the most explosive runs  originated from heavily shorted stocks  and schwartz exited their positions and  it's no different with the dillards once  the pandemic rolled around countless  hedge funds went all in on shorting  recovery stocks you know the restaurants  the cruise lines the airlines the hotels  and of course the retailers most of  these bets played out very well as many  of these businesses straight up filed  for bankruptcy but among these  short-sighted plays the hedge funds  overlook some gems like dillard's and  this would come back to haunt them as  you all probably already know we saw a  massive rise in heavily shorted stocks  near the beginning of 2021 most of the  momentum and hype went into gamestop and  amc but naturally many investors who had  missed out on these plays flocked other  heavily shorted stocks we saw dozens of  posts about dillard's stock or dds pop  up on wall street bets and the dds was  able to recover to pre-pandemic levels  through the gamestop price but it wasn't  until the second round of short squeezes  led by amc that dillards truly went  parabolic now the scc claims that the  ryzen amc and the gamestop was mostly  fueled by momentum as opposed to an  actual short squeeze but regardless of  the specific reason the amc and the  gamestop runs were extremely beneficial  to dillards unlike most meme stocks that  stalled out after the momentum died  though the alerts has continued their  exponential rise thanks to the supply  shortage  most of us associate the supply shortage  with the technology and lumber but the  supply shortage has also carried into  the fashion industry as well now the  alerts hasn't exactly pumped up prices  to the moon like nvidia but they have  been able to heavily cut back on  discounts which carries directly into  their bottom line for most of the 2010s  the alerts net margins hovered at just  under five percent right before the  pandemic taylor's net margins were  actually declining towards two percent  and it of course went negative during  the pandemic but since the start of 2021  dillard's margins have sharply recovered  and reached record levels of 10  most analysts didn't expect such numbers  whatsoever the analyst consensus for  third quarter earnings was 1.93 cents  per share but dillards came in with an  astounding 9.81 cents or 5 times the  expected amount as you would guess this  is the primary factor behind dealer's  recent 100 run in november the alerts  has given back most of these gains over  the past few weeks but many argue that  this is simply a great buying  opportunity the alerts has proven strong  resilience from the pandemic they  control a lot of real estate and they  have pretty strong financials also even  though the revenue has recovered  significantly it's not even back to  pre-pandemic levels yet currently  they're about 5 to 10 away from  pre-pandemic levels and given that a lot  of the competition has dropped out since  the pandemic it's very possible that  dillards is able to outgrow their former  self it should also be noted that even  after a 14x run the lyrids is only worth  5.5 billion dollars meanwhile lululemon  pulls in roughly the same profit but  they're worth 10 times more coming in at  53 billion dollars the lyrics doesn't  have nearly as much hype as lululemon  but considering lululemon's valuation  it's not crazy to think that dillards  could grow to a 15 to 20 billion dollar  evaluation at the same time though many  aren't nearly as convinced by dillards  here's the thing the alerts may have  strong fundamentals but at the end of  the day they're still just a strong  company in a dying industry year after  year balls are seeing less and less foot  traffic and people are becoming more and  more willing to buy everything online  dillard's is also decently competitive  on the online side of things but if  e-commerce is the future it makes little  sense to operate such massive stores  when most customers are just coming in  to pick up an order or return an item  something else they consider is that  even though dealers will face less  competition as competitors drop out  their real estate may also drop in real  value virtually all of dillard stores  are located in malls which garner their  appeal from offering customers a wide  range of shopping options but as  competitors go out of business there  will be less tenants looking to lease or  buy property in these malls the  properties will continue to go up in  nominal value due to inflation but it's  very possible that they start to lose  real value in the upcoming years also  real estate itself has gone parabolic  over the past two years so as real  estate cools down dealer's balance sheet  may start to look less bulletproof but  by far the biggest concern is the supply  shortage resolving right now the markets  are extremely inefficient but this  should just be temporary many analysts  argue that it's just a matter of time  until dillards is forced to be more  lenient with their discounts and their  profits start to fall again to  pre-pandemic levels but anyway at the  end of the day dillards was in a  relatively strong position when the  pandemic hit they owned their stores and  they were leaning up their business  combine this with the meme stock craze  and the supply shortage and dillards was  able to skyrocket 14x but though  dillards has strong financials right now  it's still heavily debated if dillards  will be able to carry on this positive  momentum or if this is simply their last  hurrah before their eventual demise  which camp are you in comment that down  below also drop a like if you think that  shopping for clothes in person is way  better and of course consider joining  our discord community to suggest future  video ideas and consider subscribing and  see more questions logically answered  but until then i'm hari and i'll see you  guys on the next one

